[2026-03-14T22:31:56.805Z] {"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01QuJsbNbfjA1i6QNqwe23Me","type":"tool_result","content":"# Replay MCP Documentation\n\n**Replay MCP** is a tool for inspecting Replay.io recordings, which capture complete browser execution histories. The documentation outlines three main categories of tools:\n\n## Exploratory Tools\nThese discover what happened and when:\n- **Errors**: Identify uncaught exceptions and React errors\n- **Timeline**: Show events including console messages, storage access, network requests, and React renders\n- **Sources**: Locate and examine JavaScript source files with execution hit counts\n\n## Explanatory Tools\nThese clarify why events occurred:\n- **Dependencies**: Track cause-and-effect relationships between events\n- **Details**: Provide context including stack frames, DOM element information, component trees, and variable values\n\n## Key Methodology\nThe documentation recommends: \"Frame your task in the form of a question that you need to answer,\" then use exploratory tools to identify related events, explanatory tools to understand causes, form hypotheses supported by data, and explain conclusions with evidence.\n\n## Useful Approach\nThe guide emphasizes that \"The Logpoint tool is extremely useful\" for tracking expression values across multiple executions and identifying unexpected patterns during investigation."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"82728290-c351-4d4c-b4fe-7b89f455d93a","uuid":"38939d9d-7b25-4bcb-81a7-a3590b2f2b69","tool_use_result":{"bytes":4555,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# Replay MCP Documentation\n\n**Replay MCP** is a tool for inspecting Replay.io recordings, which capture complete browser execution histories. The documentation outlines three main categories of tools:\n\n## Exploratory Tools\nThese discover what happened and when:\n- **Errors**: Identify uncaught exceptions and React errors\n- **Timeline**: Show events including console messages, storage access, network requests, and React renders\n- **Sources**: Locate and examine JavaScript source files with execution hit counts\n\n## Explanatory Tools\nThese clarify why events occurred:\n- **Dependencies**: Track cause-and-effect relationships between events\n- **Details**: Provide context including stack frames, DOM element information, component trees, and variable values\n\n## Key Methodology\nThe documentation recommends: \"Frame your task in the form of a question that you need to answer,\" then use exploratory tools to identify related events, explanatory tools to understand causes, form hypotheses supported by data, and explain conclusions with evidence.\n\n## Useful Approach\nThe guide emphasizes that \"The Logpoint tool is extremely useful\" for tracking expression values across multiple executions and identifying unexpected patterns during investigation.","durationMs":3125,"url":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/replayio/skills/refs/heads/main/skills/replay-mcp/SKILL.md"}}
[2026-03-14T22:31:57.253Z] {"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_017WZzEpfWcWdyvdn9ihcHLL","type":"tool_result","content":"# Replay CLI Reference\n\n**Replay CLI** is a tool for recording application sessions to gather performance data and debug issues. Here's what you need to know:\n\n## Quick Start\n\nInstall globally via npm, yarn, pnpm, or bun, then authenticate by setting your `REPLAY_API_KEY` environment variable (obtainable from app.replay.io settings).\n\n## Key Commands\n\nThe main operations include:\n- **record [url]** – Launch Replay Browser and capture a session\n- **upload [ids...]** – Send recordings to the cloud\n- **list** – View your local recordings\n- **remove [ids...]** – Delete recordings\n- **login/logout** – Manage account access\n- **info** – Check installed versions\n\n## Typical Workflow\n\n1. Run `replayio record https://myapp.com` to start recording\n2. Use the browser normally, then close it when done\n3. Upload via `replayio upload <id>` or let the prompt guide you\n4. Access your recording through the returned URL\n\n## Advanced Features\n\nYou can upload source maps for enhanced debugging with `upload-source-maps`, and integrate the Replay MCP Server with Claude for deeper analysis of recorded sessions using the provided configuration command.\n\nFor detailed options on any command, append `-h` or `--help`."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"82728290-c351-4d4c-b4fe-7b89f455d93a","uuid":"29f8a7e4-8fbe-4047-ac9a-ac2a9d8504e2","tool_use_result":{"bytes":4854,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# Replay CLI Reference\n\n**Replay CLI** is a tool for recording application sessions to gather performance data and debug issues. Here's what you need to know:\n\n## Quick Start\n\nInstall globally via npm, yarn, pnpm, or bun, then authenticate by setting your `REPLAY_API_KEY` environment variable (obtainable from app.replay.io settings).\n\n## Key Commands\n\nThe main operations include:\n- **record [url]** – Launch Replay Browser and capture a session\n- **upload [ids...]** – Send recordings to the cloud\n- **list** – View your local recordings\n- **remove [ids...]** – Delete recordings\n- **login/logout** – Manage account access\n- **info** – Check installed versions\n\n## Typical Workflow\n\n1. Run `replayio record https://myapp.com` to start recording\n2. Use the browser normally, then close it when done\n3. Upload via `replayio upload <id>` or let the prompt guide you\n4. Access your recording through the returned URL\n\n## Advanced Features\n\nYou can upload source maps for enhanced debugging with `upload-source-maps`, and integrate the Replay MCP Server with Claude for deeper analysis of recorded sessions using the provided configuration command.\n\nFor detailed options on any command, append `-h` or `--help`.","durationMs":4514,"url":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/replayio/skills/refs/heads/main/skills/replay-cli/SKILL.md"}}
